  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nA persistent area of high pressure will remain over the western \nUnited States through the weekend. The position of the high through \nWednesday will allow for a shallow influx of moisture into the lower \ndeserts, which may taper daytime highs but elevate overnight lows. \nThe high is expected to strengthen Thursday through Saturday, \nbringing widespread moderate HeatRisk to the Inland Empire and \nmountains and areas of major HeatRisk to the deserts. \n\nHigh temperatures through Wednesday will generally be within a few \ndegrees of average. Thursday through Saturday highs will be above \naverage near the coast to 5 to locally 10 degrees above average for \nsome inland areas. Low temperatures will also run above average, \nmost noticeable in the mountains and deserts where lows will be 10 \nto 15 degrees above average. For the Inland Empire and mountains, \nthe widespread moderate HeatRisk indicates that folks who are \nsensitive heat, especially those without consistent\ncooling/hydration, will be most impacted. In the deserts, major \nHeatRisk indicates anyone may see negative impacts from the heat \nif precautions aren't taken. Make sure to stay hydrated and if \npossible avoid strenuous activity during the hottest parts of the \nday (10 AM to 4 PM). \n\nFor Sunday through Tuesday, the high pressure will begin to slowly \nweaken with the flow aloft becoming more southeasterly. This would \nallow for some additional influx of monsoonal moisture into the area \nand bring a 15 to 20 percent chance of showers and thunderstorms to \nthe mountains each afternoon. Ensemble guidance show precipitable \nwater values and instability peaking Sunday and Monday. Current \nforecast follows the NBM which has chances each afternoon, with \nmost widespread coverage Sunday and Monday.",
